oo:\n\n```sh\n# fresh dataset\n% dl create test3\n[INFO   ] Creating a new annex repo at /tmp/test3 \ncreate(ok): /tmp/test3 (dataset)                                                             \n% cd test3\n\n# install two datasets from GIN as subdatasets\n% dl install -d . -s git@gin.g-node.org:/mih/datalad-test0.git test0\n[INFO   ] Cloning git@gin.g-node.org:/mih/datalad-test0.git into '/tmp/test3/test0' \ninstall(ok): test0 (dataset)                                                                 \naction summary:\n  add (ok: 1)\n  install (ok: 1)\n  save (ok: 1)\n% dl install -d . -s git@gin.g-node.org:/mih/datalad-test1.git test1\n[INFO   ] Cloning git@gin.g-node.org:/mih/datalad-test1.git into '/tmp/test3/test1' \ninstall(ok): test1 (dataset)                                                                 \naction summary:\n  add (ok: 2)\n  install (ok: 1)\n  save (ok: 1)\n\n# add freshly create repo on GIN as a remote\n% dl siblings add -s gin --url git@gin.g-node.org:/mih/datalad-test2.git\n[INFO   ] Failed to enable annex remote gin, could be a pure git or not accessible \n[WARNING] Failed to determine if gin carries annex. \n.: gin(-) [git@gin.g-node.org:/mih/datalad-test2.git (git)]\n\n# publish\n% dl publish --to gin --transfer-data all \n[INFO   ] Publishing \u003cDataset path=/tmp/test3\u003e data to gin \n[INFO   ] Publishing \u003cDataset path=/tmp/test3\u003e to gin \npublish(ok): . (dataset) [pushed to gin: ['a68e897..b1c1545', '[new branch]']]               \n\n# recursive installation of all datasets\n% dl install -r -s git@gin.g-node.org:/mih/datalad-test2.git test2clone\n[INFO   ] Cloning git@gin.g-node.org:/mih/datalad-test2.git into '/tmp/test2clone' \ninstall(ok): /tmp/test2clone (dataset)                                                       \n[INFO   ] Installing \u003cDataset path=/tmp/test2clone\u003e recursively \n[INFO   ] Cloning git@gin.g-node.org:/mih/datalad-test2.git/test0 [1 other candidates] into '/tmp/test2clone/test0' \n[INFO   ] Cloning git@gin.g-node.org:/mih/datalad-test2.git/test1 [1 other candidates] into '/tmp/test2clone/test1' \naction summary:                                                                              \n  install (ok: 3)\n\n# all file content\n% dl get -r .\n[INFO   ] Installing \u003cDataset path=/tmp/test2clone\u003e recursively \nget(ok): /tmp/test2clone/test0/testfile2.png (file) [from origin...]                         \nget(ok): /tmp/test2clone/test0 (directory)\nget(ok): /tmp/test2clone/test1/dummy (file) [from origin...]                                 \nget(ok): /tmp/test2clone/test1 (directory)\naction summary:\n  get (notneeded: 4, ok: 4)\n  install (notneeded: 2)\n```\n\nBingo!","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"2dac4f722acee11b16847c65b4c86de93f009e1f"},"timestamp":1569339639,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDUzNDYxNzkxNQ==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/2680#issuecomment-534617915"},"message":"Hi Michael.  I've been catching up with the progress of this issue and I'd like to chime in.\n\n\u003e Adding a new file (actually the same file under a different name) and publishing it to GIN works nicely. The second file is annex'ed (although identical), so the decision annex or no for the initial upload must have been made by server-side logic and is not reflected in the dataset itself -- worth keeping in mind)\n\nWe have a general rule that files over 10 MiB are annexed automatically.  This is a rather arbitrary threshold and we could apply some smarter filters based on filetype, but I find it's a decent and simple heuristic.  I understand that there may be a lot of situations where files might be \u003c 10 MiB and appropriate for git-annex versioning instead, so this is [configurable in the GIN client](https://gin.g-node.org/G-Node/Info/wiki/GinCliConfig#filtering-files-to-git-or-git-annex).  For web-uploads this can't be configured.\nOf course, if you're adding files to the repository directly using git or git-annex commands, everything should work as expected.\n\nIf you have any questions I can answer directly, let me know here.  I realise the datalad\u003c-\u003egin integration has been a long standing issue that I haven't been keeping up with but I'd like to help move this forward faster now.","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1569770698,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDUzNjMxMjU5OQ==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/2680#issuecomment-536312599"},"message":"Thank you for the feedback!\n\n\u003e Of course, if you're adding files to the repository directly using git or git-annex commands, everything should work as expected.\n\nJust FTR: I have noticed that you allow putting much larger content straight into Git. I cloned a dataset which was very slow (was suspecting the wifi), and later on my machine felt rather sluggish. Turned out this this was the problem:\n\n```\n% du -sh .git\n4.0G    .git\n% du -sh .\n30G     .\n% git annex info\ngit-annex: First run: git-annex init\n```\n\nWorking with this dataset is not very practical. Not sure what would be a good method to force users to use git annex other than limiting the total size of git objects.","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"2dac4f722acee11b16847c65b4c86de93f009e1f"},"timestamp":1569771830,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDUzNjMxNDE2NQ==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/2680#issuecomment-536314165"},"message":"Yes, we don't enforce any of the limits.  The 10 MiB filtering is done on the web uploader and in the default gin-cli configuration.  Beyond that, users can create repositories as they like.  If a user creates a git repository without annex and `git add`s a 10 GiB file and pushes, we don't block it in any way.\n\nI suppose one (not sure if good, bad, or neutral) method would be a server-side pre-receive hook that checks file sizes against a threshold.  On failure it could return a message to the pusher with instructions on how to annex the large file.\n\nWe haven't had much trouble with repositories getting out of hand like that so far, so our policies are rather lax.  I'll certainly consider it, though.\n\nIs the repository you came across the gin-cli-releases one?  I purposefully kept that one git-only for (no-longer relevant) simplicity reasons.","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"bfb0c87a087f143d5d4fb3c3b303347e581f98d0"},"timestamp":1569777582,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDUzNjMyMjIwMg==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/2680#issuecomment-536322202"},"message":"I find our rule for datalad datasets created with `-c text2git` which adds .gitattributes rule to place only text files (according to libmagic assessment) into git working out quite well . So far I saw it not being sufficient only for large xml files produced by some tools.","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"2dac4f722acee11b16847c65b4c86de93f009e1f"},"timestamp":1569835130,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDUzNjQ3Nzg0Mw==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/2680#issuecomment-536477843"},"message":"That seems like a nice default for the GIN client (and web uploads).  I'll add it to the planned feature list, thanks.  I'm still considering a hard policy for git file sizes, but I'll have to think more about it.","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"bfb0c87a087f143d5d4fb3c3b303347e581f98d0"},"timestamp":1569846914,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDUzNjU0MDg4MA==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/2680#issuecomment-536540880"},"message":"I think it might be feasible to relatively quickly get a largest object/blob size (@kyleam ?). That might be an easier to warrant limiting factor indicative of a data file being committed to git","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"2dac4f722acee11b16847c65b4c86de93f009e1f"},"timestamp":1569922903,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDUzNjk1NzA2MA==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/2680#issuecomment-536957060"},"message":"Is it slow to iterate through the sizes of new blobs during a push?","files":null},{"type":6,"author":{"id":"2dac4f722acee11b16847c65b4c86de93f009e1f"},"timestamp":1569922903,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDE1OlVzZXJDb250ZW50RWRpdDI4NjA1OTEwMQ=="},"target":"2388e40ad1db464fd57ba4314484e652fc615389ae69380ab79973c9b6956baa","message":"Is it slow to iterate through the sizes of new blobs during a push (pre-receive)?","files":null},{"type":6,"author":{"id":"2dac4f722acee11b16847c65b4c86de93f009e1f"},"timestamp":1569922910,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDE1OlVzZXJDb250ZW50RWRpdDI4NjA1OTE3Nw=="},"target":"2388e40ad1db464fd57ba4314484e652fc615389ae69380ab79973c9b6956baa","message":"Is it slow to iterate through the sizes of new blobs during a push (pre-receive)?","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"607ac2a9afabc0e44debe80e34283e413e009770"},"timestamp":1570043261,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDUzNzEzMTY2MQ==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/2680#issuecomment-537131661"},"message":"\u003e Is it slow to iterate through the sizes of new blobs during a push (pre-receive)?\n\nI'd guess that comes down to `rev-list --objects $old..$new` followed by a batched cat-file and some processing of that output.  I don't expect that'd be too slow, though perhaps you'd feel it for an initial push with a long history.\n\nWith a newer git on the server, it might be a bit faster to avoid the cat-file call using --filter/--filter-print-omitted.  Something like\n\n```sh\n#!/bin/sh\n\nlimit=20 # bytes, ridiculously low limit for testing\n\nabove_limit () {\n    git rev-list --objects --filter=\"blob:limit=$limit\" \\\n        --filter-print-omitted \"^$1\" \"$2\" | \\\n        grep -q \"^~\"\n}\n\nwhile read oldref newref refname; do\n    if test \"$oldref\" = \"0000000000000000000000000000000000000000\"\n    then\n        oldref=\"4b825dc642cb6eb9a060e54bf8d69288fbee4904\"\n    else\n        oldref=\"$oldref\"\n    fi\n\n    if above_limit \"$oldref\" \"$newref\"\n    then\n        echo \"Blob above $limit bytes found in $oldref..$newref\" 2\u003e\u00261\n        exit 1\n    fi\ndone\n```","files":null},{"type":6,"author":{"id":"607ac2a9afabc0e44debe80e34283e413e009770"},"timestamp":1570043261,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDE1OlVzZXJDb250ZW50RWRpdDI4NzAzNDEyMg=="},"target":"6aadc1f556296993f8c77bef592f33a98283bee86db2c4630efbad4e0ad16ae4","message":"\u003e Is it slow to iterate through the sizes of new blobs during a push (pre-receive)?\n\nI'd guess that comes down to `rev-list --objects $old..$new` followed by a batched cat-file and some processing of that output.  I don't expect that'd be too slow, though perhaps you'd feel it for an initial push with a long history.\n\nWith a newer git on the server, it might be a bit faster to avoid the cat-file call using --filter/--filter-print-omitted.  Something like\n\n```sh\n#!/bin/sh\n\nlimit=20 # bytes, ridiculously low limit for testing\n\nabove_limit () {\n    git rev-list --objects --filter=\"blob:limit=$limit\" \\\n        --filter-print-omitted \"^$1\" \"$2\" | \\\n        grep -q \"^~\"\n}\n\nwhile read oldref newref refname; do\n    if test \"$oldref\" = \"0000000000000000000000000000000000000000\"\n    then\n        oldref=\"4b825dc642cb6eb9a060e54bf8d69288fbee4904\"\n    fi\n\n    if above_limit \"$oldref\" \"$newref\"\n    then\n        echo \"Blob above $limit bytes found in $oldref..$newref\" 2\u003e\u00261\n        exit 1\n    fi\ndone\n```","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"2dac4f722acee11b16847c65b4c86de93f009e1f"},"timestamp":1570011626,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDUzNzQzMDkxMA==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/2680#issuecomment-537430910"},"message":"That seems very reasonable to me.  I'd definitely experiment with some extreme/edge cases before moving forward.  Thanks @kyleam.","files":null}]}